Notes: Each of the 20 corpus papers is a short controlled study comparing a retrieval-augmented answering condition against a parametric-only condition on a single benchmark (e.g. LawFactQA, CodeRAG-Bench, MedRAG-Bench), measured with a factual-accuracy or hallucination metric. They split into two groups with opposite stated conclusions: 8 papers (S003, S004, S007, S009, S012, S013, S014, S019) conclude retrieval augmentation improves factual accuracy, while 12 papers (S001, S002, S005, S006, S008, S010, S011, S015, S016, S017, S018, S020) conclude it does not. The corpus as a whole is therefore mixed/inconclusive. One internal inconsistency: S001's abstract states 'Retrieval augmentation does not measurably reduce hallucinated content' but its results table shows the retrieval-augmented model at 17.8% hallucinated content versus 29.5% for the parametric-only model. Recorded claims: one per paper quoting its abstract conclusion sentence; S00N maps to claim-00000N (S001->claim-000001 ... S020->claim-000020).
